    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The one window that gains. A "
        },
        {
          "text": "major",
          "marks": ["italic"]
        },
        {
          "text": " pentatonic in this same span holds ten dots; A minor's holds twelve. It's the only one of the five windows where the count differs by two, and the only one where minor is richer by more than one note. That's the chapter-2 edge effect showing up again at this layer: the window's edges are fixed frets, and here more of the minor scale's notes happen to fall inside them. (Three of the other four windows hold twelve dots in "
        },
        {
          "text": "both",
          "marks": ["italic"]
        },
        {
          "text": " qualities, so don't generalise from this one.)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Chord tones and the rest. Seven are "
        },
        {
          "text": "Am",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ": "
        },
        {
          "text": "6·0, 5·0, 5·3, 4·2, 3·2, 2·1, 1·0",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". Five are not: "
        },
        {
          "text": "6·3, 4·0, 3·0, 2·3, 1·3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". Both roots are "
        },
        {
          "text": "5·0",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and "
        },
        {
